 "path": ".openclaw/tmp/continual-arc-rectangle-rays.md",
 "content": "# Continual-ARC note: singleton-directed rays through a solid rectangle

Observed in task-54cad0a1 (three demo pairs, test solved): the grid contains one solid rectangle of color 2 and singleton markers of another color. Preserve every original cell/marker. For a singleton aligned with a row or column of the rectangle, extend the rectangle's color along that row/column *through the rectangle and away from the singleton*, continuing from the far side of the rectangle to the grid boundary. Do not fill the gap between the singleton and the rectangle; leave the singleton unchanged. If multiple aligned markers/rays apply, union the extensions. In examples, a marker left of the rectangle extends that row rightward beyond the rectangle; a marker right of it extends leftward; markers above/below similarly extend the affected columns away from the marker. Rows/columns without an aligned singleton keep the rectangle's original extent. Validate ray orientation on each marker and retain markers as their original color.

Evidence: demos 1–3 each show horizontal and vertical extensions in this manner; the task test was solved after an initial incorrect guess. The rule was consistent across varying rectangle sizes, marker colors, and grid dimensions."
